On 2009-01-03, RjY <RjY@sp.am> wrote:
> Martin Read posted:
>>You don't need MC3 in Spork or vanilla, [...]
> Could you or David explain why this is? I am under the impression that
> magic cancellation protects you from so many irritating special types of
> attack (draining and sliming being two obvious ones) that I can't see
> why anybody would want to go without the highest level of it.

The qualifying word here is 'need'.  I would agree with the statement
that MC3 is not a 'need' in the same sense that reflection and MR are a
'need'; however, it is certainly my own preference.

Displacement is MC2, which provides 60-something% protection against
special touch- (and touch-like) attacks, and provides an additional
chance of those attacks missing in the first place.

Taking the cases of draining and sliming into account:

-- By the time slimes appear, you should have both plenty of AC and a
decent ranged attack as an option; they are slow and can be handled
simply by beating them to death _or_ using your token ranged attack.
The chances of them hitting you in the first place are minimal to begin
with.  There is also the ability to zap yourself with a wand of fire or
read a scroll of fire to take care of incidental sliming; the length of
time it takes to become completely slime means that just saving your
scrolls of fire from earlier in the dungeon will provide plenty of ammo
for the few 'saves' you may need from having only MC2.

-- Draining levels isn't much of a fear, since you will get one of those
levels back by simply killing the drainer.  After a certain point, XL
are only gained via wraith corpses, gain level potions, and the like, so
it isn't an actual problem to have lost all the illusory 'progress'
granted by the XP between levels; you aren't going that route anyway.
This leaves the only real major drain threat as something which can hit
you _twice_ during a single combat and drain you both times; MC2 and
your natural AC should both deter this sufficiently.

Me?  I wear MC3 because at lower levels things like werefoo are a royal
pain in the ass, and I don't like wasting other defenses or recovery
methods against them.

Later in the game, the options I generally wear anyway (robe, cloak of
MR, oilskin, even protection) are MC3 and thus it doesn't matter;
displacement just isn't good enough for me to bother swapping out and
taking the extra hit, since so few monsters can see invisible 
(specifically, air elementals).

